---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Placeholder from '../components/Placeholder.astro';
import { url } from '../config';
---

<Base
  title="How It Works"
  description="The Anchor Point owner experience: onboarding survey, systems inventory, maintenance schedule, and monthly reporting."
>
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="shell">
      <p class="eyebrow">How it works</p>
      <h1>Four steps from handover to routine.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        The first few weeks are the work. After that, management should be
        something you notice only when the monthly report arrives.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell prose">
      <p class="eyebrow">Step 01</p>
      <h2>Onboarding survey</h2>
      <p>
        We meet you at the vessel and walk it together. The purpose is to
        establish an honest baseline: what condition the boat is in today, what
        has been deferred, and what needs attention first.
      </p>
      <p>
        You tell us how you use the boat — weekends in the harbour, long
        offshore runs, guests aboard, or laid up for part of the year. That
        usage pattern is what makes the maintenance schedule realistic rather
        than theoretical.
      </p>
      <ul class="ticks">
        <li>Walkthrough of the vessel with the owner</li>
        <li>Current condition recorded and photographed</li>
        <li>Deferred and outstanding items identified up front</li>
        <li>Usage pattern and owner priorities captured</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Typical timing: <Placeholder key="onboardTiming" label="how long onboarding takes from first walkthrough to active management" />
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--alt">
    <div class="shell prose">
      <p class="eyebrow">Step 02</p>
      <h2>Systems inventory</h2>
      <p>
        We record every major system aboard with its make, model, serial number,
        current hours, and warranty status. Engines, generator, watermaker,
        HVAC, electronics, hull and running gear.
      </p>
      <p>
        This is the unglamorous step that makes everything afterwards work. A
        maintenance schedule is only as good as the equipment list behind it,
        and it is the thing most owners discover they have never had written
        down in one place.
      </p>
      <ul class="ticks">
        <li>Make, model, and serial captured per system</li>
        <li>Engine and generator hours logged as the tracking baseline</li>
        <li>Warranty windows recorded before they lapse unnoticed</li>
        <li>The inventory belongs to you and travels with the vessel</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell prose">
      <p class="eyebrow">Step 03</p>
      <h2>Maintenance schedule</h2>
      <p>
        With the inventory in place, each system gets its service intervals.
        Those intervals run on two clocks — calendar time and engine hours —
        and whichever comes first is what triggers the work.
      </p>
      <p>
        Each item carries lead time ahead of its due date, so haul-outs, bottom
        jobs, zinc changes, oil services, and survey deadlines surface while
        there is still time to book a yard slot rather than after the window
        has closed.
      </p>
      <ul class="ticks">
        <li>Intervals assigned per system from the manufacturer's schedule</li>
        <li>Dual tracking on calendar date and engine hours</li>
        <li>Lead-time alerts before an item becomes due</li>
        <li>Work orders raised, dispatched, and tracked to completion</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="notice">
        <p>
          Intervals come from manufacturer schedules and Anchor Point's
          operating standard.
          <Placeholder key="intervalSource" label="confirm the authority Anchor Point cites for service intervals" />
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--alt">
    <div class="shell prose">
      <p class="eyebrow">Step 04</p>
      <h2>Monthly report</h2>
      <p>
        Each month you receive a written report: what was completed, what is
        scheduled next, the current hours and condition of your systems, and
        every cost incurred that period — with photographs of the work.
      </p>
      <p>
        The intent is that you can read it in three minutes and know the full
        state of your vessel, and that if you ever want to check a claim about
        what was done, the record is already there.
      </p>
      <ul class="ticks">
        <li>Work completed, with photo evidence</li>
        <li>Work scheduled for the coming period</li>
        <li>Current engine hours and system condition</li>
        <li>Itemised costs and management fee</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Report delivery: <Placeholder key="reportDelivery" label="how and when the monthly report is delivered — e.g. emailed PDF on the first of the month" />
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--dark">
    <div class="shell center">
      <h2>Start with a walkthrough.</h2>
      <p class="lede">
        Send us your vessel details and we'll arrange a time to see the boat at
        your slip or mooring.
      </p>
      <div class="btn-row">
        <a class="btn btn--primary" href={url('/contact')}>Request a Consultation</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
